Zend MM chunks and huge blocks from a pool of aligned arenas on WASI (wasix-php).

The memory manager maps 2 MiB chunks with mmap and trims the slack to align
them with partial munmaps; huge blocks shrink with munmap and grow with
mremap or a fixed mmap. On WASI, mmap is wasi-libc's emulation over malloc:
its munmap only takes a whole mapping (EINVAL otherwise), so every trim
failed and leaked up to a chunk, and nothing could grow in place.

Chunks and huge blocks (rounded up to whole chunks, as on Windows) come from
a pool of 2 MiB-aligned arenas instead, taken from libc with posix_memalign
(8 MiB, then about the pool's size, at most 32 MiB). Free space is an
address-ordered, coalesced list of chunk runs: a huge block shrinks by
giving its tail back and grows in place when the run after it is free
(otherwise zend_mm_realloc_huge copies, as upstream does when mremap
fails). Memory freed back to the pool serves later chunks; a wasm memory
never shrinks, so arenas aren't returned to libc. A chunk is zeroed when
it is handed out, as a fresh mapping is.

With posix_memalign per chunk instead, each chunk cost about 4 MiB of wasm
memory (its alignment slack), so a script with a 120 MiB heap grew the
memory to 252 MiB.

